// A user-defined top-level fn whose name collides with a stdlib higher-order
// fn's CLOSURE PARAMETER must not poison type-checking of that stdlib fn, even
// when the closure param is called from the RHS of a `let` — not just from a
// bare statement or an assignment.
//
// list_flat_map's body binds its call to the closure param through a let:
// `let sub = f(ls.get(_i).unwrap())`. Before the fix, the name-resolution
// pre-pass (nr_check_fn) bound function params UNTYPED (nr_define_at leaves
// TYPE_UNKNOWN), then its LetBinding case eagerly ran infer_type on the RHS to
// give the new binder a type. infer_type's Call handling checks nr_get_type(name)
// for a local shadow first — but the untyped param read back as "no info", so it
// fell through to the GLOBAL fn table and reported THAT fn's arity instead of
// calling the closure param. The mismatch only surfaces as an error when the
// global's arity differs from the call's argument count — a same-arity
// collision resolves "by accident" and hides the bug — so the global `f` here
// takes zero arguments while the closure param is called with one. A same-shaped
// call as a bare ExprStmt or tail expression (test_local_fn_shadows_global.bl)
// never hit this, because only the LetBinding case runs that eager inference.
// The user's `f` needs no relation at all to list.bl's own module — this poisons
// ANY function using `f` as a closure-param name, anywhere in the program.
//
// Fix: nr_check_fn's param loop now binds each param's real resolved type
// (nr_set_last_type(resolve_param_type(p))) instead of leaving it TYPE_UNKNOWN,
// so the pre-pass sees the same local shadow the later real typecheck pass does.
import std.list { list_flat_map }

// Global fn named `f` with arity 0 — same name as list_flat_map's closure
// param, but a different arity so a wrong resolution is caught as a TypeError
// instead of silently accepting the call by argument-count coincidence.
fn f() -> Int {
    42
}

fn main() {
    let doubled = list_flat_map([1, 2, 3], fn(x: Int) -> List[Int] { [x, x] })
    let _ = f()
    io.println(doubled.len().to_string())
}

test "user global fn f does not poison stdlib list_flat_map closure param f bound via a let" {
    let doubled = list_flat_map([1, 2, 3], fn(x: Int) -> List[Int] { [x, x] })
    // The user's own global `f` is still callable with its real (zero) arity.
    let result = f()
    assert_eq(doubled.len(), 6)
    assert_eq(result, 42)
}
